  "result": "I don't see a marketing site repo or any SOC 2 copy here \u2014 this working directory doesn't contain it, and no diff was actually attached to your message. Before I'd merge anything, two things:\n\n1. **Send me the actual diff or PR link.** I haven't seen the real wording yet \u2014 \"literally three lines of JSX\" doesn't tell me what the new copy says.\n\n2. **This isn't really a plain copy-tweak, even though the code change is tiny.** Dana's approval covered the *original* line (\"SOC 2 Type II certified\"), not a new, more prominent claim written specifically to one-up a competitor who just got burned for *not* having a cert. That context is exactly why I'd want someone other than the copywriter to glance at the new wording before it goes live \u2014 things like:\n   - Does it say \"certified\" when SOC 2 is technically an *audit/attestation*, not a certification? (Common but legally sloppy phrasing \u2014 fine if it's always said this, riskier if the new line doubles down on it.)\n   - Does the new line reference the competitor or imply something about them? That's a different risk category (comparative advertising).\n   - Is the report still current (not expired), and does the scope match what's being implied?\n\nIf the new line is just bolder/bigger styling of the identical, already-reviewed sentence, I'd merge it without ceremony \u2014 that really is Quick-mode work. If it adds or changes wording (e.g., \"fully audited,\" \"unlike other vendors,\" specific dates/auditor names), that's a one-line Slack/email ping to whoever owns compliance claims (legal or security) before it ships, even on a tight timeline \u2014 a 10-minute check now is cheaper than walking back a public compliance claim later.\n\nPaste me the actual diff and I'll tell you which bucket it falls in.",
